        "text": "CIR scenarios represent CL environments where repetition is naturally present in the stream. Our experiments provide a number of different insights that, for the first time, focus solely on the role of repetition in CL environments. Although the concept of repetition is quite intuitive, it is not obvious how to realize it in practice for research purposes. We proposed two CIR generators that address this issue. The generators are able to create (even unlimited) streams with repetition from a single dataset.\nWe showed that, unlike CI scenarios, knowledge accumulation happens naturally in CIR streams, even without applying any CL strategy. Both the model performance (measured by the MCA) and the model similarity (measured by the CKA) increase over time. The systematic repetition provided by Replay, where each iteration includes examples from all seen experiences, provides an advantage but it is not critical. Even random repetition is sufficient to induce knowledge accumulation. We found that existing Replay strategies are exclusively designed for classical CI scenarios. Thus, we propose a novel strategy, ER-FA, to exploit the properties of streams with repetition. ER-FA is able to accumulate knowledge even when the stream is highly unbalanced in terms of class frequency, with few classes appearing very rarely. Our ER-FA surpasses by a large margin other Replay approaches when monitoring the accuracy for infrequent classes. It also preserves accuracy for the frequent ones. Overall, ER-FA guarantees a more robust performance on a wide range of real-world scenarios, where classes are not homogeneously distributed over time. Our study also indicates other strategies which look particularly promising for CIR scenarios. Distillation-based strategies, like LwF, seem to benefit from the repetition present in the stream. When learning in scenarios with a moderate amount of repetition, LwF may even constitute a better solution than Replay. Characterizing the type and amount of repetition becomes therefore fundamental in order to choose an adequate CL strategy.\nThe empirical results about CIR provided in this work open new research directions which depart from the existing ones, mainly focused on the mitigation of forgetting in CI scenarios. We hope that our results will promote the study of CIR scenarios and the development of new CL strategies, able to exploit the inner semantics of repetition, a natural trait of real-world data streams, in many other clever and imaginative ways."
